CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a single three-dimensional diagram of a closed surface carrying outward normal vectors. A pale blue solid shaped like a rectangular prism with bulging faces and rounded edges occupies the center, set in a frame whose z axis rises at the upper left, y axis extends right, and x axis comes forward to the lower left. The letter E labels the solid at its middle and the letter S labels the boundary near its upper right. Short dark blue arrows, each labeled N, project outward from the boundary: two from the top, two from the bottom, one from the left face, one from the right face, and one drawn on the upper surface angling up and back. Longer unlabeled orange arrows run in parallel through and around the figure from lower left to upper right, standing for the ambient vector field. Dashed curves mark the hidden rear edges. Setting the labeled normals against the uniformly directed field arrows shows at a glance how much of the field crosses the boundary at each face. The diagram accompanies a result tying the flux of a vector field across a closed surface to a triple integral of its divergence over the solid inside.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Coordinate frame: z axis rising at upper left with label 'z', y axis extending to the right with label 'y', x axis coming forward to the lower left with label 'x', each drawn as a thin black line with a small arrowhead
  - Closed solid E: pale blue semi-transparent rectangular-prism-like body with outward-bulging faces and rounded edges, centered on the origin, long dimension along y
  - Boundary surface S drawn as the visible bulging shell of the solid with darker blue edge curves along the rounded corner seams
  - Dashed blue curves marking the hidden rear vertical edge and hidden rear bottom/left edges of the solid
  - Label 'E' in italic at the middle of the solid (on the front face)
  - Label 'S' in italic near the upper right of the solid, just outside the top-right corner
  - Dark blue outward normal arrow from the top-left region of the top face, pointing straight up, labeled 'N'
  - Dark blue outward normal arrow from the top-right region of the top face, pointing straight up, labeled 'N'
  - Dark blue outward normal arrow drawn on the upper surface angling up and back (toward +x/up), labeled 'N'
  - Dark blue outward normal arrow from the left (−y) face pointing left, labeled 'N'
  - Dark blue outward normal arrow from the right (+y) face pointing right, labeled 'N'
  - Dark blue outward normal arrow from the bottom face near the center pointing down through the solid, labeled 'N'
  - Dark blue outward normal arrow below the bottom face at the right pointing straight down, labeled 'N'
  - Seven long parallel orange arrows of the ambient vector field, all pointing from lower-left to upper-right, some passing through the solid and some passing outside it (left of, above and right of the body)

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World axes match the figure: +Y is screen-right (the 'y' axis), +Z is screen-up (the 'z' axis), +X comes forward toward the lower-left (the 'x' axis). Solid E is centered at approximately (0, 0.3, 0.2) and measures about X 2.2 (depth) × Y 4.0 (length, the long horizontal dimension) × Z 1.8 (height); all six faces bulge outward by ~0.18 and the twelve edges are rounded with radius ~0.25 (build as a superellipsoid/rounded box, e.g. |x/a|^4+|y/b|^4+|z/c|^4=1). Axes: z from 0 up to 3.2, y from 0 right to 4.8, x forward to 3.0, each thin black (#111) with a cone arrowhead and italic serif label at the tip. The origin sits just behind the lower-left corner of the solid so the axes pass near/through the solid's left rear; the hidden rear vertical edge and the hidden bottom edges are drawn as light dashed blue (#5b8fc0) curves. Colors: solid fill pale steel blue #b3c9de at ~0.55 opacity with darker blue seam/edge curves #2f6193; normal arrows dark navy #1f4e79, length ~0.9, shaft 0.035 radius, each with an italic bold 'N' label at the tip; field arrows orange #e8622a, length ~2.4, shaft 0.04, all parallel with unit direction ≈ (0.20, 0.80, 0.56) (normalized) i.e. rising to the upper right; arrows arranged on a loose 3×3 lattice offset in x and z so three pass through the solid and four pass outside it (upper-left, upper-right, lower-left, lower-right). Labels: 'E' italic at the front face center near (1.1, 0.1, 0.25); 'S' italic just outside the top-right corner near (0.4, 2.6, 1.6). Normal placements: two on the top face at y≈-1.2 and y≈+1.1 pointing +Z; one on the top face near (0.3, 0.3, top) angled up-and-back toward (0.5,0.2,0.85); one on the −Y face pointing −Y; one on the +Y face pointing +Y; two on the bottom face (y≈-0.3 and y≈+0.9) pointing −Z. Flux sign per patch with this field: +Y face and top face positive, −Y face and bottom face negative, +X (front) face slightly positive, −X (back) face slightly negative.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "That E is a genuine 3D solid and S is its closed boundary; rotating shows which faces the uniform orange field enters and which it exits."
  },
  {
    "id": "selectFace",
    "type": "click",
    "teaches": "Selecting a patch of S brightens its outward normal N and the nearby field arrows, and reports F·N as positive (field exits), negative (field enters), or near zero (field grazes the patch) — the local flux contribution."
  },
  {
    "id": "viewMode",
    "type": "toggle",
    "teaches": "That the flux of F through the boundary S equals the accumulated divergence throughout the solid E — the same quantity read two ways.",
    "label": "Emphasis",
    "options": [
      "Boundary flux through S",
      "Divergence inside E"
    ],
    "default": "Boundary flux through S"
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "The closed surface S bounding E",
    "narration": "The pale blue solid E is enclosed by the surface S. Short dark blue arrows N stick out of every face, always pointing away from the interior.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "Boundary flux through S"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Where the field exits and enters",
    "narration": "The orange arrows form a uniform field running lower-left to upper-right. Click the right and top patches — F·N is positive there, so the field leaves E. On the left and bottom patches F·N is negative: the field enters.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "Boundary flux through S"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Accumulation inside the solid",
    "narration": "Switching emphasis to E fades the boundary arrows and shades the interior by divergence. Net outflow across S equals the total divergence piled up inside — the divergence theorem.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "Divergence inside E"
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "heightFraction": 0.62
}

NOTES: Build the solid as a superellipsoid mesh (SphereGeometry vertices remapped, or a rounded-box with bulge displacement) using MeshPhysicalMaterial, transparent opacity 0.55, depthWrite false, side DoubleSide, plus a matching darker-blue wireframe of only the 12 rounded corner seams (extract via curves, not full wireframe). Hidden rear edges as LineDashedMaterial lines that are drawn with depthTest disabled so they read as dashes through the body. Normals and field arrows as ArrowHelper-style groups (CylinderGeometry shaft + ConeGeometry head) so individual arrows can be recolored on click. Labels ('x','y','z','E','S','N') as sprite/CSS2D italic serif text that always faces the camera. Click picking: attach invisible raycast proxy patches (slightly offset spherical caps) to each of the six face regions; on selection set that face's patch to a highlight tint, brighten its N arrow to #0d3a63, brighten the 2-3 nearest orange arrows to #ff7a3c and dim all others to 0.25 opacity, and display an F·N sign readout. Toggle: 'Boundary flux' state shows full-opacity normals and arrows with the shell edges emphasized; 'Divergence inside E' state fades the normals and exterior arrows to 0.15, raises solid opacity, and renders an interior scalar shading (nested translucent iso-shells or a point cloud colored by div F) to suggest accumulation. Keep OrbitControls with damping, target at the solid center, no zoom-to-extremes (min/max distance clamp).

